WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.440
Protesters in Budapest usually start here
at the Hungarian parliament.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:05.410
This afternoon, I meet a couple of students
preparing for an anti-Orban demonstration.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:11.440
"Hungary has experienced a wave of protests
in recent days and normally it’s young people

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.300
like Eva, like Gaspar who come here to the
Kossuth square to say "Elég volt," 'It’s

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.300
enough'.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.840
What is enough?

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.260
"There are certain amounts of things that
we have experienced, the destruction of health

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.510
care, the destruction of education, or the
slave-law which is going to make people work

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.439
overtime, 400 hours.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:34.699
The whole society is collapsing on us.

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:37.379
It is suffocating us on all levels."

00:00:37.379 --> 00:00:42.059
Unlike past anti-government protests, recent
ones have not ended in Budapest.

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:44.359
They have shifted to the countryside.

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:50.640
We are in Nyiregyhaza, in the Northeast - one
of the poorest regions of Hungary and a stronghold

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.170
of Fidesz, the governing party.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:58.789
I want to ask people about the new labor law
that has triggered the protests.

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:04.120
It allows companies to ask their employees
to work 400 hours overtime every year.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.140
But no one wants to talk on camera, afraid
they may lose their jobs.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.390
I meet Attila Varga, who may not be afraid,
but he's angry.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:18.890
His job is to drive 500 km through Hungary
every night for a shipping company.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:25.590
"I start my job every night at eight o'clock
and have to sign that I have finished at 4:30

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.590
in the morning.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:27.860
They make us commit fraud."

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:33.090
In fact, he says he works 12 to 14 hours every
night.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.660
For 400 euros a month.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.800
"The government says there are possibilities.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.420
That’s not true.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.510
There aren’t many jobs.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:45.410
That’s why you are forced to accept working
overtime."

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:50.740
Attila is concerned that employees will be
exploited even more and he is taking his anger

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.490
onto the streets.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:57.730
Hundreds of others have come to the city center
to protest at what they call "a slave labor

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:58.730
law."

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:02.890
"It will be right here where we kick out the
first brick of the wall that represents the

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.490
system of Viktor Orban."

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:12.520
But the organizers, different opposition parties,
call for a peaceful protest.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.710
They don't want clashes with the police.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:19.010
"They are chanting 'Orban out,' 'We don’t
want your slave law.'

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:24.954
This crowd here is very energized because
all of a sudden the very much fragmented opposition,

00:02:24.954 --> 00:02:28.380
the opposition parties are united against
Orban.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.340
And they want to keep this momentum alive."

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.800
It’s freezing, way below zero.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.370
The protesters here are determined to fight
in the streets beyond Christmas.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:45.300
The big question is how many people will support
this new wave of resistance.

